{
  "url": "/scales",
  "title": "Piano Scales: Notes, Patterns and Practice References",
  "description": "Compare major and minor scale notes, explore other scale types, and check the exact form before practicing.",
  "user_task": "Choose a scale and understand its notes, form, fingering availability and next practice action.",
  "questions": [
    "Which notes belong to my scale?",
    "How do natural, harmonic and melodic minor differ?",
    "What do scale degrees mean?",
    "Which hand, direction and range does a fingering cover?",
    "What can I listen to or print?"
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "start",
      "heading": "Piano scales: choose a sound, then a starting note",
      "body": "Start with C major: C–D–E–F–G–A–B–C. Choose another tonic to compare its notes. The final C repeats the first note one octave higher; it is not an eighth distinct scale degree.",
      "source_ids": [
        "AC-01",
        "AC-04"
      ],
      "evidence_status": "[已核实]"
    },
    {
      "id": "formula",
      "heading": "Read the pattern",
      "body": "For a major scale, move by whole, whole, half, whole, whole, whole, half steps. A whole step spans two adjacent-key steps; a half step spans one. Keep the note spelling shown for the chosen key. Fifteen conventional major-key spellings in this chart represent twelve tonic pitch classes on an equal-tempered keyboard.",
      "source_ids": [
        "AC-01",
        "AC-04"
      ],
      "evidence_status": "[推断] 由核实音程及本表15行音高类集合计算"
    },
    {
      "id": "minor",
      "heading": "Choose the minor form",
      "body": "A natural minor uses A–B–C–D–E–F–G. Harmonic minor raises G to G♯. Classical melodic minor raises F and G on the way up and returns to the natural-minor notes on the way down. Select the form before comparing, listening, or printing.",
      "source_ids": [
        "AC-02"
      ],
      "evidence_status": "[已核实]"
    },
    {
      "id": "compare",
      "heading": "Same notes or same tonic?",
      "body": "C major and A natural minor share a note collection but organize it around different tonics. C major and C minor start on the same tonic; C natural minor lowers E, A and B to E♭, A♭ and B♭.",
      "source_ids": [
        "AC-02"
      ],
      "evidence_status": "[已核实]"
    },
    {
      "id": "degrees",
      "heading": "Give each note a job",
      "body": "In C major the degrees are C tonic, D supertonic, E mediant, F subdominant, G dominant, A submediant and B leading tone. In natural minor, degree seven is a whole step below the tonic and is called the subtonic.",
      "source_ids": [
        "AC-01",
        "AC-02"
      ],
      "evidence_status": "[已核实]"
    },
    {
      "id": "jazz",
      "heading": "A few jazz scale examples",
      "body": "Compare C Dorian (C D E♭ F G A B♭), C Mixolydian (C D E F G A B♭), and C blues (C E♭ F G♭ G B♭). Bebop dominant adds a major seventh to Mixolydian; C B B♭ A G F E D C gives a descending example. The choice of notes still depends on the musical context.",
      "source_ids": [
        "AC-03"
      ],
      "evidence_status": "[已核实]"
    },
    {
      "id": "types",
      "heading": "Continue with a different type",
      "body": "Use the modes page to compare seven diatonic modes. Pentatonic and blues pages show their own note collections. Chromatic practice moves through every neighboring key. Harmonic major has its own reference page; it is not another name for harmonic minor.",
      "source_ids": [
        "AC-03"
      ],
      "evidence_status": "[推断] 根据已规划类型页组织导航；定义以对应页来源为准"
    },
    {
      "id": "practice",
      "heading": "Try one comparison",
      "body": "Pick two examples and name the notes that change. Play one hand at a time at a pace you can follow. Keep the tonic visible in the result. When a fingering is available, use only its stated hand, direction and octave range; a blank field is not an invitation to reuse another scale’s fingering.",
      "source_ids": [],
      "evidence_status": "[推断] 原创编辑练习与数据使用约束"
    },
    {
      "id": "print",
      "heading": "Print the selected reference",
      "body": "The print view should name the tonic, scale form, direction and range, and show exactly the notes in the result. Finger numbers belong on the printout only when that combination has been checked. See the individual scale reference for its available fingering.",
      "source_ids": [],
      "evidence_status": "[推断] 待实现功能文案，非已上线声明"
    }
  ],
